    <h1>Jared Kushner's $2 Billion Saudi Investment, Middle East Diplomacy Role</h1>
    <p class="date">April 2022 – Present</p>
    <p>
      Jared Kushner, President Trump's son-in-law, secured a $2 billion investment from the Saudi Arabian government in his private equity firm Affinity Partners in 2021. During Trump's first term, Kushner was a senior advisor to the president and acted as a diplomatic representative for the White House to the Middle East. During this time, Kushner developed close ties to Mohammed bin Salman, the crown prince of Saudi Arabia, who sanctioned the financing of the deal. Affinity Partners also raised $1.5 billion from Gulf governments like Qatar and the United Arab Emirates, states where Kushner conducted government business during the first Trump Administration. In total, 99% of Affinity's funding has come from foreign investments.
    </p>
    <p>
      Saudi Arabia's massive financing for a member of Trump's family and former envoy of the American government is a serious conflict of interest. During his role in the White House, Kushner extensively defended Mohammed bin Salman against international criticism, including for his CIA-confirmed role in the murder of American journalist Jamal Khashoggi. Kushner advocated internally against punitive measures against the crown prince and helped shield him from consequences in the immediate aftermath of the killing. It was reported in 2018 that the crown prince openly boasted that Kushner was "in his pocket." Saudi Arabian officials were reportedly concerned about the investment, given Kushner had zero experience in private equity, but MBS pushed the deal through regardless.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kushner's relationship with the crown prince has deepened since. In 2025, Kushner secured the largest private buyout in history, purchasing video game company Electronic Arts with financial backing from the Saudi sovereign wealth fund Public Investment Fund. The deal placed a major American media and technology company under effective Saudi influence, with a member of the Trump family as the vehicle. In 2024, Kushner promised he would make no fundraising efforts during a second Trump presidency. In Trump's second term, Kushner has continued to act as an envoy of the Trump administration and has attempted to solicit a further $5 billion from governments and sovereign wealth funds in the Middle East during his diplomatic efforts. While Kushner is helping negotiate in matters like the end of the Iran war, he is also asking governments to make private contributions to his company.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conflict of interest here is not subtle or a matter of interpretation. A member of the Trump family elicited and continues to seek private financial gain while acting in an official capacity with the United States government — using his public role to enormously enrich himself. Beyond the monetary implications, Kushner's allegiance to a foreign government is also a grave national security concern. The question of whether Kushner's diplomatic conduct is shaped by the prospect of that money — or whether the money is a reward for services already rendered — is textbook public corruption. In any other social-political climate, be it in a different decade or country, such an infraction would be plainly criminal.
    </p>

